{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792102657,"nonce":"tGv0BiH5/NfwamdWMeuOqmY/1jQ=","title":"AOT Haxe method names shadow libc symbols","message":"Native UtilityProcess hangs collecting stdout after its child exits successfully. A sample shows haxe_fileinput_read_all blocked in read_to_end/read; lsof shows the parent retains both ends of all three child pipes. nm reports a defined global _close in the executable: a Haxe close method shadows libc close used by Rust file-descriptor cleanup.\n\nReproduce by compiling ProcessPipes.hx to a native executable and running it, or running the native UtilityProcess putEnv/getEnv case. JIT ProcessPipes passes because runtime libc bindings come from the host.\n\nNamespace Haxe definitions after LLVM bodies/references are complete, preserving C ABI symbols and generated startup hooks. Include native ProcessPipes and standard-stream/empty-string regressions in the AOT CI check. The native executable must exit and collect all child output without retaining pipe writers.","files":null}]}